ata-i="56"/><line x1="200" y1="14" x2="200" y2="30" transform="rotate(342 200 200)" class="tk" data-i="57"/><line x1="200" y1="14" x2="200" y2="30" transform="rotate(348 200 200)" class="tk" data-i="58"/><line x1="200" y1="14" x2="200" y2="30" transform="rotate(354 200 200)" class="tk" data-i="59"/><g id="hm"><line x1="200" y1="200" x2="200" y2="70" class="hh"/></g><g id="mm"><line x1="200" y1="214" x2="200" y2="34" class="mh"/></g><circle cx="200" cy="200" r="5" class="hub"/></svg><div class="mn-hero-i rv rv-c"><img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1716399201049-60835e27c843?auto=format&fit=crop&w=520&h=520&q=75" alt="a white watch dial in close-up" width="520" height="520" fetchpriority="high"></div></div></div></section>

<section class="mn-s mn-sixty"><div class="mn-w mn-sixty-g"><p class="mn-big rv">There are <b data-count="1440">1440</b> minutes in a day. A good watch keeps every one of them, give or take a few seconds.</p><div class="mn-sixty-n stg"><div class="rv"><b data-count="60">60</b><span>marks on a minute track</span></div><div class="rv"><b data-count="8">8</b><span>beats a second in many automatic watches</span></div><div class="rv"><b data-count="10">10</b><span>seconds a day: often the gap between a good and an ordinary mechanical watch</span></div></div></div></section>

<section class="mn-sw"><div class="mn-w mn-sw-g"><div class="mn-sw-l"><div class="mn-sw-st"><div class="mn-sw-im on" data-i="0"><img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1623304741393-54f6c11c0da6?auto=format&fit=crop&w=760&h=900&q=75" alt="a watchmaker working on a movement with tweezers" width="760" height="900" loading="lazy"></div><div class="mn-sw-im" data-i="1"><img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1679134015859-6281e4382065?auto=format&fit=crop&w=760&h=900&q=75" alt="a watchmaker looking through a loupe" width="760" height="900" loading="lazy"></div><div class="mn-sw-im" data-i="2"><img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1580837428500-74aa7eefc672?auto=format&fit=crop&w=760&h=900&q=75" alt="a minimalist watch on a wrist" width="760" height="900" loading="lazy"></div><div class="mn-sw-im" data-i="3"><img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1758887953059-ca6f8e454207?auto=format&fit=crop&w=760&h=900&q=75" alt="a black watch with white hands" width="760" height="900" loading="lazy"></div><div class="mn-sw-im" data-i="4"><img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1661086381638-f973ca589bb8?auto=format&fit=crop&w=760&h=900&q=75" alt="a diver's watch resting on sand" width="760" height="900" loading="lazy"></div><p class="mn-sw-c" id="sw-c">00:00</p></div></div><div class="mn-sw-r"><p class="mn-ey">One minute, five stages</p><h2 class="rv">How a minute is made</h2><div class="mn-sw-s" data-i="0"><span>00:00</span><h3>The movement</h3><p>A balance wheel swings back and forth, usually eight times a second, slicing time into equal parts.</p></div><div class="mn-sw-s" data-i="1"><span>00:15</span><h3>The regulation</h3><p>The watchmaker adjusts the balance so the watch gains or loses only a few seconds a day.</p></div><div class="mn-sw-s" data-i="2"><span>00:30</span><h3>The dial</h3><p>A minute track of sixty fine marks turns that regularity into something you can read.</p></div><div class="mn-sw-s" data-i="3"><span>00:45</span><h3>The hands</h3><p>A long minute hand reaches the track; a short hour hand points at the numerals.</p></div><div class="mn-sw-s" data-i="4"><span>01:00</span><h3>The wearer</h3><p>And at the end of all that care, someone glances down and knows exactly where they are in the day.</p></div></div></div></section>

